(*!
<summary>Creates independent enumerators for traversing a collection.</summary>
<description>
The collection is borrowed by its enumerators and must remain alive throughout their use. Enumerator ownership
is separate from ownership of the collection and its elements.
</description>
*)

INTERFACE IEnumerable EXTENDS IObject]]></Declaration>
    <Method Name="CreateEnumerator" Id="{0977fb9b-9046-4f07-b4fd-b067d11a88d4}">
      <Declaration><![CDATA[(*!
<summary>Creates a new dynamically allocated enumerator with its own traversal state.</summary>
<description>
Each call creates an independent cursor positioned before the first element. It may be used across PLC
cycles while its source collection remains alive and its traversal contract is respected. Copying the
interface creates an alias. Releasing the enumerator leaves the collection and element ownership intact.
<note type="information">
The caller owns the enumerator. Release it explicitly through <c>Memory.TryReleaseDynamicObject</c> using an
<c>IObject</c> interface variable. Cycle cleanup does not release the enumerator. Discard any other cursor
aliases after disposal, including after a failed disposal attempt that clears the interface variable.
</note>
Allocation failure invokes the shared terminal allocation-failure mechanism without creating a heap
exception. A null enumerator is not a recoverable failure result. The application boundary must enforce the
fatal latch.
</description>
*)
METHOD CreateEnumerator : IEnumerator]]></Declaration>
    </Method>
  </Itf>
</TcPlcObject>